(centre of the world, and that gold would be rained therefrom upon) Tj T*
(fortunate Scotland. The whole nation went almost frantic with the) Tj T*
(fever, for Panama, with its gold mines and its world-wide trade,) Tj T*
(was going to make Scotland rich beyond the dreams of avarice. It is) Tj T*
(estimated that nearly half the capital of the country was sunk in the) Tj T*
(Darien scheme.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Chartered by the Scottish Parliament in 1695, three vessels sailed) Tj T*
(from Leith in July, 1698, with some twelve hundred settlers on board,) Tj T*
(Paterson and his wife among the number. All Edinburgh flocked down to) Tj T*
(Leith to wish the members God-speed, and then returned to their homes) Tj T*
(to dream of the streams of gold with which Scotland was to be flooded.) Tj T*
(In a few years everybody would be rich, and Edinburgh would be the) Tj T*
(greatest and proudest city in the world. Trade, however, was destined) Tj T*
(to flow to a city a little farther south.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The scheme proved a dismal failure. England and Holland opposed the) Tj T*
(new colony; the East India Company treated it as a rival, and Spain) Tj T*
(was actively hostile. The climate did the rest. Before the close of) Tj T*
(1699 "New Edinburgh" was deserted, and the colonists, decimated by) Tj T*
(want of provisions and disease, set sail for New York. To make matters) Tj T*
(worse, a second company meanwhile had sailed from Scotland, where the) Tj T*
(utmost enthusiasm still prevailed; but the new arrivals found the town) Tj T*
(deserted, and themselves at the mercy of the Spanish warships. Mad with) Tj T*
(rage at the lack of success of their national adventure, the Scotch) Tj T*
(openly accused the English Government of treachery, declaring that its) Tj T*
(conduct in withholding food supplies was as discreditable to it as was) Tj T*
(the butchery of Mac Ian and his clan at Glencoe in 1692, when neither) Tj T*
(old man nor child was spared, and fugitives were allowed to perish of) Tj T*
(hunger and exposure in the mountains.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Paterson's faith in Panama must have been profound. His wife died) Tj T*
(in the new colony, and he himself suffered severely in health; yet,) Tj T*
(after his return towards the end of 1699, directly his health began) Tj T*
(to improve, we read of his approaching William with a fresh Darien) Tj T*
(venture. The King naturally refused to risk a second disaster, and) Tj T*
(Paterson, like all great speculators who have risked everything and) Tj T*
(lost, could not again persuade the public to share his enthusiasm, for) Tj T*
(that mysterious entity seldom trusts a man after a cloud has obscured) Tj T*
(his "star." Once his spell of so-called good luck is broken, the public) Tj T*
(desert him in a body, when the adventurer, if he be wise, retires into) Tj T*
(obscurity with his spoil.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Paterson lived to discover that it is only a rising star, radiating) Tj T*
(success, that can obtain a sufficiently large following to finance) Tj T*
(a great scheme, and though he strove manfully to promote the new) Tj T*
(venture, his sanguine predictions were received sceptically. Nor did) Tj T*
(his subsequent schemes meet with a better reception. But he must still) Tj T*
(have retained some influence, for, after the Act of Union in 1707,) Tj T*
(he was returned to Parliament by a Scotch burgh. His chief claim to) Tj T*
(distinction, however, undoubtedly rests upon the fact that he founded) Tj T*
(the Bank of England, of which he was appointed one of the first) Tj T*
