(The Bank of England, from its inception down to the present day, has) Tj T*
(never been a Government institution. It was originally simply a company) Tj T*
(that advanced money to and transacted business for the Government,) Tj T*
(which, in return, granted it certain privileges and concessions; but) Tj T*
(the connection between the Government and the Bank was so close, and) Tj T*
(their interests so identical, that public opinion connected the one) Tj T*
(indissolubly with the other. From this conception sprang the erroneous) Tj T*
(impression that the Bank is a Government establishment, when, in) Tj T*
(reality, it is no more so than is the National Provincial Bank of) Tj T*
(England or the London and County Bank.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1694, the Government of William III., which was generally in a state) Tj T*
(of monetary tightness, found that the war with France was draining) Tj T*
(its resources, and, having failed to raise sufficient funds by the) Tj T*
(imposition of taxes, it resolved, apparently as a kind of _dernier) Tj T*
(ressort_, to accept Paterson's financial scheme, which had been shelved) Tj T*
(some three years earlier; and on 27th July, 1694, a charter was granted) Tj T*
(to the "Corporation of the Governor and Company of the Bank of England.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The capital of the company, г1,200,000, was subscribed by some forty) Tj T*
(London merchants, and lent to the Government. It is only reasonable to) Tj T*
(assume that the subscribers were supporters of the Government, and that) Tj T*
(they were Whigs, whose aim, in supplying William with the sinews of) Tj T*
(war, was the crushing of James, whose pusillanimity had disgusted even) Tj T*
(his own followers at the battle of the Boyne in 1690.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then, again, the commercial morality of the Stuarts was notoriously bad) Tj T*
(in the City. Charles I., when the City of London refused him a loan,) Tj T*
(took forcible possession of г200,000 deposited by the Goldsmiths in) Tj T*
(the Exchequer; and Charles II., in 1672, robbed them of considerably) Tj T*
(over г1,000,000. The Goldsmiths, in those days, were the private) Tj T*
(bankers with whom the London merchants left their cash, receiving an) Tj T*
(acknowledgment or receipt in return, promising payment on demand, and) Tj T*
(the Goldsmiths deposited their surplus cash in the Exchequer, just as) Tj T*
(the banks of to-day do with the Bank of England. Through this act of) Tj T*
(spoliation the Goldsmiths were unable to meet their liabilities, and) Tj T*
(many of them, together with their customers, were involved in common) Tj T*
(ruin in consequence. James II. added to the financial sins of his house) Tj T*
(by debasing the currency: so small wonder that the merchants of London) Tj T*
(had had enough of the Stuarts, whose theory of the "Divine right" of) Tj T*
(kings did not even stop short at the pockets of their subjects--always) Tj T*
(their most vulnerable point.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Bank of England, which to-day is quite outside party politics, was) Tj T*
(at its inception a Whig finance company, incorporated solely for the) Tj T*
(purpose of lending its capital to the Government at the rate of eight) Tj T*
(per cent. per annum; and out of this creation has evolved the present) Tj T*
("Old Lady of Threadneedle Street," whose career, if chequered, has been) Tj T*
(one of unquestionable integrity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
