(It is difficult even in imagination to picture to oneself the England) Tj T*
(of 1694; but it is easy to understand that in those days great) Tj T*
(storehouses of capital were non-existent--non-existent, that is to) Tj T*
(say, in the modern sense. Our huge credit institutions, which are) Tj T*
(indispensable in the twentieth century for the proper carrying on of) Tj T*
(trade, and which dive by means of branches into almost every corner of) Tj T*
(the land, thereby collecting millions of pounds of loanable capital,) Tj T*
(would have spread their tentacles in vain during the seventeenth) Tj T*
(century, when neither the money nor the facilities for its profitable) Tj T*
(employment existed in the country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Capital was scarce--consequently the rate of interest was high--and) Tj T*
(eight per cent. was a rate at which even the Government could not) Tj T*
(borrow in the City in 1694, from ten to thirteen per cent. per annum) Tj T*
(being about the value of loanable capital, while the commission paid) Tj T*
(was oftentimes exorbitant. The Bank, which was established by the) Tj T*
(Whigs, was naturally bitterly opposed by the Tories, who saw in its) Tj T*
(success the destruction of the cause they had at heart. The capitalist) Tj T*
(class disliked it for selfish reasons; and the Goldsmiths, recognising) Tj T*
(a formidable opponent, joined issue with its enemies.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Holders of stock and everybody connected with the Bank were looked upon) Tj T*
(as enemies of the House of Stuart, which, were it restored to power,) Tj T*
(would naturally wreak its vengeance upon a company that had helped to) Tj T*
(finance William--for forgiveness is one of those abstract attributes) Tj T*
(with which only brave and wise men are blest, and James II. had not) Tj T*
(given proof of possessing either courage or wisdom. Small wonder then) Tj T*
(that the City should support the Dutchman.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The National Debt, too, was founded during the reign of William, the) Tj T*
(first loan of г1,000,000 being raised in 1693, and those persons who) Tj T*
(held it were bound by the strongest of ties--commercial ties--to) Tj T*
(William. The fund-holders were Liberal; the Bank was Liberal; and as) Tj T*
(its very life was dependent upon the existence of the Government, it) Tj T*
(seems only natural that, in the popular mind, it should have been) Tj T*
(looked upon as a Government institution, though there is but little) Tj T*
(excuse for so classing it now. The fact that so many people still share) Tj T*
(this illusion, however, clearly proves that a large proportion of the) Tj T*
(public is unacquainted with the Bank's history.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Bank of England's charter was renewed in 1697, and again in 1708,) Tj T*
(when, in order to prevent the establishment of similar institutions,) Tj T*
(it was granted the monopoly of Joint Stock Banking in England. This it) Tj T*
(retained until 1826, when an Act was passed permitting the formation) Tj T*
(of Joint Stock Banks of Unlimited Liability beyond sixty-five miles of) Tj T*
(London, provided they had no branches in the Metropolis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is a long jump from 1708 to 1826, and, of course, the charter was) Tj T*
(renewed many times between the two dates, the Government generally) Tj T*
(taking advantage of each extension to force some concession from the) Tj T*
(Bank, which, as its credit and business expanded, had increased its) Tj T*
(original capital by many millions; but 1826 was the year of reform, and) Tj T*
