(the intervening period possesses little interest except to the student.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Between 1826 and 1829 the Bank opened eleven provincial branches, but) Tj T*
(those which were established at Gloucester, Swansea, Exeter, and) Tj T*
(Norwich have since been closed. Joint Stock Banks were then started in) Tj T*
(the provinces, though not with very happy results, for in 1832 their) Tj T*
(reckless trading was severely stigmatised by Lord Overstone; but it) Tj T*
(was not until 1834 that the first joint stock bank, the London and) Tj T*
(Westminster, was started in London, a clause having been inserted in) Tj T*
(the Act when the charter of the Bank of England was renewed in 1833, to) Tj T*
(the effect that, provided a joint stock bank did not issue notes, it) Tj T*
(was at liberty to carry on business in the City.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Both the Bank of England and the London private bankers opposed the) Tj T*
(new bank with acerbity, the former refusing to open an account for it) Tj T*
(in its books, and the latter declining to admit it into the Clearing) Tj T*
(House. Not satisfied with this, the Bank brought an action against the) Tj T*
(Westminster. But it was quite natural that the newcomer should have) Tj T*
(been received in this fashion, for innovations, however necessary and) Tj T*
(useful, are seldom accepted rapturously in this country, which appears) Tj T*
(to have almost a Chinese dislike of the unusual. Besides, it is not the) Tj T*
(custom of the country, even for the sake of appearances, to receive a) Tj T*
(trade rival with open arms, and it would have been a little surprising) Tj T*
(had the Bank surrendered its monopoly of joint stock banking in) Tj T*
(England without a struggle, whilst its desire, after being stripped of) Tj T*
(some of its privileges, to annoy its despoilers, was, if not laudable,) Tj T*
(eminently human.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1836 the London Joint Stock Bank followed the example of the) Tj T*
(Westminster, and in 1839 the Union Bank of London, which has recently) Tj T*
(amalgamated with Messrs. Smiths, opened its doors, while such) Tj T*
(well-known banks as the National Provincial Bank of England and the) Tj T*
(London and County Bank were formed in 1833 and 1836 respectively. The) Tj T*
(trade of the country had by that time far outgrown the resources of the) Tj T*
(Bank of England, which was quite unable to minister to the increasing) Tj T*
(demands of a prosperous and progressive England; and to-day the only) Tj T*
(monopoly which the Bank enjoys is that left to it by the Act of 1844.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From William and Mary to Victoria, in whose reign the Act of 1844--that) Tj T*
(Magna Charta of the banking community--was introduced, covers a most) Tj T*
(interesting period in the history of the nation, whose development had) Tj T*
(been retarded by the "Divine right" of the Stuarts, which cost Charles) Tj T*
(I. his head and James II. his throne. The theory is much in evidence) Tj T*
(to-day, though it now takes the form of a great abstract idea, not) Tj T*
(compatible with practical politics, and which has found a resting place) Tj T*
(in the heart, rather than in the head, of the people--for the practical) Tj T*
(twentieth century has a strange trick of banishing disproved theories) Tj T*
(from the head to the heart; and perhaps it is this national trait which) Tj T*
(saves the country from violent revolutions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It would be a mistake to assert that commerce had declined under) Tj T*
(the Stuarts. It increased rapidly in spite of them; but, after the) Tj T*
