(We have seen that the history of the Bank of England may be divided) Tj T*
(into two periods. From 1708 to 1826 the Bank enjoyed the monopoly of) Tj T*
(joint stock banking in England. After 1826 it had to adapt itself) Tj T*
(to a constantly changing environment. England, in fact, outgrew the) Tj T*
(Bank, just as the financial world has outgrown London. The directors) Tj T*
(of the Bank of England were City merchants, whose ideas usually run) Tj T*
(in a particular groove. It is not, therefore, in the least remarkable) Tj T*
(that they stuck to old customs and neglected new opportunities. The) Tj T*
(directors of the London and Westminster Bank made the same mistake. So) Tj T*
(did those of the Union Bank of London, the London Joint Stock Bank, and) Tj T*
(one or two others, simply because their training was of the City: that) Tj T*
(is to say, like the streets around the Bank, narrow.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To a very great extent the Bank of England is dependent upon the) Tj T*
(bankers' balances, for, unless it held them, it would not be able to) Tj T*
(finance the Government. If its directors had, however, thoroughly) Tj T*
(understood the movement of 1826, the Bank would now be a much more) Tj T*
(independent institution, and would be a power in every county in) Tj T*
(England and Wales. In 1826 the Government expressly desired the) Tj T*
(directors of the Bank to open country branches, and by 1830 it) Tj T*
(possessed eleven offices in the large provincial towns. But the) Tj T*
(innovation was not encouraged by those in authority, and to-day the) Tj T*
(Bank of England possesses only nine country and two Metropolitan) Tj T*
(branches. Unquestionably a golden opportunity was neglected, for, had) Tj T*
(the directors decided to open in the large provincial towns, Bank stock) Tj T*
(would probably be worth over five hundred at this moment.) Tj T*
